## Solution

Place the cube at $0 \le x, y, z \le L$ with $L = 10$. The temperature $u(x,y,z,t)$ satisfies the heat equation

$$\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = \kappa\, \nabla^2 u,$$

where $\kappa$ is the (constant) thermal diffusivity of the material, with boundary and initial conditions

$$u = 0 \text{ on all six faces for } t>0, \qquad u(x,y,z,0) = 100 \text{ inside the cube.}$$

---

### Separation of variables

Because the boundary data vanishes on every face and the domain is a rectangular box, the spatial eigenfunctions factorize:

$$X_n(x)Y_m(y)Z_p(z), \qquad X_n(x)=\sin\!\Big(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\Big),\quad n,m,p = 1,2,3,\dots$$

These vanish at $x=0,L$ (and similarly for $y,z$), and the corresponding eigenvalues of $-\nabla^2$ are

$$\lambda_{nmp} = \frac{\pi^2}{L^2}\bigl(n^2+m^2+p^2\bigr).$$

Hence the general solution satisfying the boundary conditions is

$$u(x,y,z,t) = \sum_{n,m,p\ge 1} A_{nmp}\,
\sin\!\Big(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\Big)\sin\!\Big(\frac{m\pi y}{L}\Big)\sin\!\Big(\frac{p\pi z}{L}\Big)\,
e^{-\kappa\lambda_{nmp}t}.$$

---

### Determining the coefficients

At $t=0$ we need

$$100 = \sum_{n,m,p\ge 1} A_{nmp}\,
\sin\!\Big(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\Big)\sin\!\Big(\frac{m\pi y}{L}\Big)\sin\!\Big(\frac{p\pi z}{L}\Big).$$

By orthogonality on $[0,L]$,

$$A_{nmp} = \Big(\frac{2}{L}\Big)^3 \int_0^L\!\!\int_0^L\!\!\int_0^L 100\,
\sin\!\Big(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\Big)\sin\!\Big(\frac{m\pi y}{L}\Big)\sin\!\Big(\frac{p\pi z}{L}\Big)\,dx\,dy\,dz.$$

The one-dimensional integral is

$$\int_0^L \sin\!\Big(\frac{k\pi x}{L}\Big)\,dx = \frac{L}{k\pi}\bigl(1-\cos(k\pi)\bigr) = \frac{L}{k\pi}\bigl(1-(-1)^k\bigr) = \begin{cases}\dfrac{2L}{k\pi}, & k \text{ odd},\\[4pt] 0, & k \text{ even}.\end{cases}$$

Therefore $A_{nmp}=0$ unless $n,m,p$ are **all odd**, and for $n,m,p$ odd,

$$A_{nmp} = \frac{8}{L^3}\cdot 100 \cdot \frac{2L}{n\pi}\cdot\frac{2L}{m\pi}\cdot\frac{2L}{p\pi}
= \frac{8\cdot 100 \cdot 8\,L^3}{L^3\,nmp\,\pi^3}
= \frac{6400}{nmp\,\pi^3}.$$

---

### Final temperature distribution

With $L=10$, the time-dependent temperature inside the cube is

$$\boxed{\;u(x,y,z,t) = \sum_{\substack{n,m,p\ge 1\\ n,m,p\ \mathrm{odd}}} \frac{6400}{nmp\,\pi^3}\,
\sin\!\Big(\frac{n\pi x}{10}\Big)\sin\!\Big(\frac{m\pi y}{10}\Big)\sin\!\Big(\frac{p\pi z}{10}\Big)\,
\exp\!\Big[-\frac{\kappa\pi^2}{100}\bigl(n^2+m^2+p^2\bigr)t\Big]\;}$$

for $0\le x,y,z\le 10$ and $t\ge 0$.

---

### Verification of the key properties

- **Boundary conditions.** Each sine factor vanishes at $0$ and at $10$, so $u=0$ on all six faces for every $t>0$. ✓
- **PDE.** Term-by-term differentiation gives $u_t = -\kappa\lambda_{nmp}\,(\text{term})$ and $\kappa\nabla^2(\text{term}) = -\kappa\lambda_{nmp}\,(\text{term})$, so $u_t = \kappa\nabla^2 u$. ✓
- **Initial condition.** The triple sine series of the constant $100$ on the cube reproduces $100$ at every interior point (the Fourier series of a constant converges to that constant in the interior), and the coefficients above are exactly the triple Fourier sine coefficients of the constant function $100$. ✓
- **Long-time behavior.** The leading term $(n,m,p)=(1,1,1)$ decays slowest, with rate $\kappa\pi^2\cdot 3/100$, so $u\to 0$ as $t\to\infty$, consistent with the cube cooling to the boundary temperature $0°$. ✓

